  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Pleasant conditions through tomorrow as the\nregion sits under high pressure. Lows tonight dip into the 40s\nand 50s.\n\nLarge high pressure continues to make its way over southern New \nEngland through the rest of today, ending up directly overhead \ntonight. With clear skies and light to calm winds overnight, \nradiational cooling is expected to take control, allowing low \ntemperatures to fall into the 40s and 50s tonight. Areas that \nradiate well should drop into the mid 40s while areas closer to \nthe coasts and don't radiate as well will fall into the mid to \nlow 50s overnight.\n\nHigh pressure remains affixed over the region into tomorrow,\nthen shifts off the east coast heading into the evening hours.\n850 mb temperatures remain around 10-14C in the afternoon hours, \nand highs top out in the upper 70s, with areas closer to the \ncoasts in the low 70s with local seabreezes.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers, and possibly a few thunderstorms, with \nbrief torrential rainfall possible late Sat night into the first \nhalf of Sun. Low probability for localized flash flooding. \n\nHigh pressure moves off the coast Saturday night, clearing the path \nfor a low pressure to pass through Sunday. A larger high pressure \nover central Canada and the Northern Plains should drive a cold \nfront well offshore by Monday morning. This will result in a rather \nbrief period of rainfall across southern New England. The timing for \nthe most likely period of rainfall has slowed slightly. It no \nappears to be some time from late Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning. Rainfall should end from west to east Sunday afternoon.\nLow risk for thunderstorms as well, but not expecting severe\nweather.\n\nThe most likely outcome is one half to one inch of rainfall for \nmost. However, there is a 35-50% chance for more than one inch of \nrainfall, especially across portions of eastern CT into RI. \nRegardless, it will be quite humid. Thus, localized downpours are \npossible. Will need to continue to monitor the flooding risk this \nweekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dry and seasonable Mon-Tue. A brief bout of\nsummer warmth and humidity possible Wed into Thu ahead of a \ncold front.\n\nHigh pressure builds back into the region Mon and Tue,returning dry \nand seasonable weather. This high pressure system moves east of the \nregion by the middle of next week. This may allow for a brief return \nof summerlike warmth/humidity some time Wed into Thu. There is a \nfair amount of uncertainty on the timing of another cold front Wed \nnight into Thu. With a high pressure over the North Atlantic, would \nnot be overly surprised if this timing winds up being slower with \nlater forecasts. Until this front passes us by, expecting above \nnormal temperatures for Wed into Thu.",
